---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import hoopHouse from '../assets/photos/hoop-house.jpg';
import shedSolarArray from '../assets/photos/shed-solar-array.jpg';

const duoW = [420, 700, 1100];
const duoS = '(max-width: 40rem) 100vw, 32rem';
---
<Base title="The Farm — Windrose Farm" description="The four focus areas of Windrose Farm: an organic apiary, organic gardens, a high-desert orchard, and a small vineyard — designed as one interconnected system.">
  <section class="wrap article-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Farm</p>
    <h1>One ecosystem, four gardens</h1>
    <p class="prose" style="color: var(--ink-soft);">
      Nothing at Windrose stands alone. The apiary, gardens, orchard, and
      vineyard are designed as a single interconnected system, where every
      part strengthens the others.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <div class="glyph" style="font-size: 2rem;">🐝</div>
      <h2>Apiary</h2>
      <p>
        One of the defining features of Windrose Farm — and, in a real sense,
        its heart. The goals are simple and stubborn:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Healthy honeybee colonies</li>
        <li>Pollination of the orchard and gardens</li>
        <li>Raw local honey</li>
        <li>Beeswax products</li>
        <li>Native pollinator habitat</li>
        <li>Continuous seasonal forage</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The emphasis is always on healthy bees and a healthy ecosystem. The
        bees arrive when the land is ready for them — once orchard blossoms and
        lavender can carry a colony through the season, closing the loop the
        farm's own design philosophy calls for.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prose">
    <div class="glyph" style="font-size: 2rem;">🌱</div>
    <h2>Gardens</h2>
    <p>
      Vegetables, herbs, flowers, and perennial crops grown with regenerative
      methods: compost, drip irrigation, mulching, companion planting, and
      pollinator-friendly design. The primary purpose is feeding the family
      while continually improving soil health — every season's garden should
      leave the ground better than it found it.
    </p>
    <figure class="snapshot">
      <Image
        src={hoopHouse}
        alt="The bare steel hoop frame of a greenhouse standing in open ground, its covering not yet fitted"
        widths={duoW}
        sizes={duoS}
      />
      <figcaption>
        The hoop house, abandoned by the previous owners and still standing.
        Bringing it back is part of the plan — at 5,700 feet with a 120-day
        season, protected growing space buys weeks at both ends.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <div class="glyph" style="font-size: 2rem;">🍎</div>
      <h2>Orchard</h2>
      <p>
        A protected orchard inside an elk-resistant enclosure, featuring fruit
        and nut trees selected specifically for northern Arizona's short
        seasons and alkaline soil. It integrates fruit trees, nut trees,
        pollinator habitat, nitrogen-fixing plants, perennial groundcovers,
        and beekeeping — intended as a resilient ecosystem, not simply rows
        of fruit trees.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first plantings — asparagus, jujube, and cold-hardy apples — were
        chosen because they genuinely want to live here. You can read more
        about how we chose them on <a href="/land/">The Land</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prose">
    <div class="glyph" style="font-size: 2rem;">🍇</div>
    <h2>Vineyard</h2>
    <p>
      A small vineyard, serving as both a learning experience and a source of
      grapes for small-batch winemaking. The purpose is craftsmanship,
      education, and enjoyment — not commercial production. Like everything
      here, it's an experiment we expect to learn from as much as harvest from.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The Systems Philosophy</p>
      <h2>Every system strengthens the others</h2>
      <ul class="loop-list">
        <li>Bees pollinate orchard blossoms.</li>
        <li>Orchard blossoms feed the bees.</li>
        <li>Compost feeds the gardens.</li>
        <li>Gardens support pollinators.</li>
        <li>Trees reduce wind.</li>
        <li>Healthy soils retain water.</li>
        <li>Native plants support wildlife.</li>
        <li>Solar power supports off-grid infrastructure.</li>
      </ul>
      <figure class="snapshot" style="max-width: var(--measure);">
        <Image
          src={shedSolarArray}
          alt="Solar panels mounted along the green metal roof of the red machine shed under a clear sky"
          widths={duoW}
          sizes={duoS}
        />
        <figcaption>
          The machine shed carries the array that runs the well pump, the
          irrigation timers, and eventually the barn.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
